WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.572 --> 00:00:01.738
[grunting]

00:00:04.583 --> 00:00:07.120
[air whooshing]

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.690
Narrator: These behind-the-scene clips

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.150
are what's known as reference footage.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.880
Reference footage is used
in animation all the time

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.980
to make sure animated
characters' movements,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.940
like walking and jumping, are realistic.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.500
Probably the most
difficult scenes to animate

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.916
are fight scenes,

00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:25.524
like the extremely complex head-to-heads

00:00:25.524 --> 00:00:29.130
in "Avatar: The Last Airbender"
and "The Legend of Korra."

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.840
Animator: Instead of just going
on the ground on his butt,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.275
he actually drops down, and he's like,

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:35.253
[blows] on his finger. There's, like, air.

00:00:35.253 --> 00:00:36.765
You know, there's, like, wind.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.550
Narrator: To animate them
meant finding someone

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.650
who could perform and choreograph

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.923
multiple martial arts styles.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.070
Sifu Kisu: It was fun.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.130
I was the only person on
the planet with my job

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:49.260
for a hot minute.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.993
Narrator: That's Sifu Kisu,
a master of martial arts

00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:54.320
and the fight choreographer
and reference actor

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.220
for "Avatar: The Last Airbender."

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.690
Kisu has over 40 years of
experience in martial arts

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.500
and has studied tae kwon
do, karate, jujitsu,

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.740
Northern Shaolin-style kung fu, and more.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.010
Kisu worked on all 61 episodes of "Avatar"

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.560
and collaborated with a team

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.520
of martial arts experts on "Korra."

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.350
In "Avatar," having a choreographer

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.890
and reference actor was
especially important

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.590
because the four kingdoms
used different fighting styles

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.770
with their own specific movements.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.421
Kisu based each distinct bending style

00:01:24.421 --> 00:01:26.050
on a different martial art.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.520
The fluid movements of
waterbending are based on tai chi,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.230
the spinning movements of
airbending are taken from bagua,

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.430
the powerful movements of firebending

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.410
are based on Northern
Shaolin-style kung fu,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.214
and the sturdy,
low-center-of-gravity movements

00:01:41.214 --> 00:01:43.998
of earthbending are based on hung ga.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.933
The first step in creating
these animated fight sequences

00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:50.590
is storyboarding, when the
artists receive a script

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:53.430
and draw rough sketches
of an entire episode.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.250
The descriptions of
fight scenes in scripts

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:58.220
typically only include the
broad strokes of the fight,

00:01:58.220 --> 00:01:59.530
so the director of the episode

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.730
will often create the
temporary choreography

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.140
during this initial phase.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.005
Giancarlo Volpe: When we
got to the actual fighting,

00:02:05.005 --> 00:02:06.670
you know, who kicks, who punches,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.370
who was the aggressor and all that,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.385
oftentimes I do something like this,

00:02:10.385 --> 00:02:12.300
where it's like, "Aang shoots air,"

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.740
and I'll just do this or something.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.730
Do a drawing that looks like that.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.880
Narrator: After storyboarding
comes choreography,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:20.940
where Kisu met with the animation team

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.480
to refine the choreography
of the fight scenes.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.840
There were usually about
five to 10 fight scenes

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.060
per episode that had to be choreographed.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.910
Kisu says that in this first meeting,

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.800
he would just pitch a series of movements

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.300
that he thought would represent
a certain type of bending.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.200
For example, Kisu said that
if a firebending technique

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.530
called for swinging, flaming fists,

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.710
he would suggest an appropriate movement,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.250
such as striking motions from
double dagger form kung fu.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.643
Other animated shows
have tried to mix magic

00:02:47.643 --> 00:02:49.640
and martial arts, but Kisu noted

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.030
that they don't often use
martial arts movements

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.355
when conjuring magic.

00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:55.981
You can see this when comparing
"Avatar: The Last Airbender"

00:02:55.981 --> 00:02:58.311
to other shows featuring
supernatural powers,

00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:00.240
like some scenes from "Dragon Ball Z."

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.147
Kisu: It's like, if I'm holding
a stone in my hand like this

00:03:04.147 --> 00:03:06.738
and it jumps out of my
hand and hits the camera,

00:03:06.738 --> 00:03:08.050
you're going to question that, right?

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.710
But if I wind up and I make the motion

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.120
and the antic of throwing that,

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.185
then that's going to read more

00:03:14.185 --> 00:03:15.650
to something you can understand, right?

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:17.380
But we thought that
that would be important,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.260
that it should come from somewhere

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:21.840
to go to somewhere.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.485
And I think that was one of the big

00:03:23.485 --> 00:03:25.580
contributing factors to the success

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.638
of "Avatar: The Last Airbender."

00:03:27.638 --> 00:03:28.930
Narrator: After Kisu and the director

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.610
agreed on the choreography,
they filmed the action.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.890
Kisu sometimes performed
the fight choreography

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.640
for two or more fighting
characters separately.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.490
Animators would later take these shots

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:42.071
and combine them into a single
fight between characters.

00:03:42.071 --> 00:03:43.890
After filming, the
artists took the footage

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.490
and used it to add more detail,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.500
like specific poses, to
their initial storyboards.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.460
The time it cost to do
the reference filming

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.630
was recouped by having

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:54.330
an extremely accurate model for drawing.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.801
Ultimately, this led to
a better final product,

00:03:56.801 --> 00:03:59.220
because the character
poses were more accurate.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.450
The storyboard team sent
the polished storyboards

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.160
back to Kisu for review.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.390
Volpe: Then it'd be sort of
a back and forth where, like,

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.820
maybe we come up with a new shot

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.590
that we never talked about
during the first meeting.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.080
Narrator: This process was
repeated two to three times

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.467
until everyone was
satisfied with the result.

00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:17.220
Before sending the finished storyboards

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.540
off to a team in Korea
for the final animation,

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.960
the director would film one
final version of the fight

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.199
with Kisu with all of the
finalized camera angles.

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:27.020
Kisu: If it was going to be a wide angle,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.473
we had these bleachers that were in, like,

00:04:28.473 --> 00:04:30.870
a little makeshift basketball
court at Nickelodeon,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.540
and guys would crawl up
there with the camera

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.290
and get a down shot.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.040
We'd change to a wide angle

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.000
and make it look like
it was really far away.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.282
Like, the fight with Zuko
and Azula on the boat dock,

00:04:41.282 --> 00:04:43.050
where she tried to scratch his face

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.610
and they had a fire battle,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.540
that was done on the
front steps at Nickelodeon

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:49.510
in front of the SpongeBob statue.

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:51.870
Narrator: Storyboards aren't
created for every frame.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.430
They're created for every
third frame of animation,

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.860
so the reference footage made
it easier for the animators

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.480
to create the in-between
frames that were missing.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.810
While having reference footage is ideal,

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.310
sometimes it was impossible
to capture accurate

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.810
live-action reference footage,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.721
mostly because the characters
possess magical powers.

00:05:08.721 --> 00:05:10.640
Some fights in the show
called for a movement

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.980
that no real human could replicate,

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.620
like this scene from "Korra."

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:15.880
In these situations,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.990
the martial artists
would split up the action

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.370
into several chunks,

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:22.170
which would be stitched
together in the final animation.

00:05:23.191 --> 00:05:24.475
Kisu: In the beginning credits

00:05:24.475 --> 00:05:26.674
for "Avatar: The Last
Airbender," when Zuko would do

00:05:26.674 --> 00:05:29.666
the big whirlwind kick with the flame

00:05:29.666 --> 00:05:30.790
and he'd come down and hit the ground,

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:33.190
at the time, I was
recovering from an injury,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:34.950
and so I couldn't do that kick.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.306
We call it a one-legged whirlwind kick.

00:05:37.306 --> 00:05:40.560
And so I'm literally
rolled over someone's back,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.130
like, someone bent over,

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.760
and then I just rolled
over that person's back.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:46.230
The opening credits for "Korra,"

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:48.660
they show, the airbender
does this crazy thing

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.200
where he kicks this way
and lays back that way.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.870
I ended up just holding Brian in my arms,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.240
which is really funny.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.000
Narrator: It's this lifelike martial arts

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.350
choreography and attention to detail

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:01.650
that make the difference between

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.275
static fight scenes, like this,

00:06:04.275 --> 00:06:06.513
and dynamic ones, like this.

